@startuml
' Hand-written EER diagram (categorical level): a true category / union type.
!include ../../_style.iuml
allowmixing
title Categorical (EER) - Finance: Payer as a category (union type) of Guardian and Sponsor

entity "Guardian\n(people.Guardians)" as Guardian
entity "Sponsor\n(finance.Sponsors)" as Sponsor
entity "Payer\n(finance.Payers)" as Payer
entity "Invoice\n(finance.Invoices)" as Invoice
entity "Payment\n(finance.Payments)" as Payment

usecase "U" as U

' Superclasses of the category: partial participation (not every guardian
' or sponsor is a payer).
Guardian -- U : partial
Sponsor -- U : partial
' Category side: total - every Payer is exactly one Guardian OR one Sponsor.
U -[thickness=3]-> Payer : subset (total)

Payer ||--o{ Invoice : is billed
Payer ||--o{ Payment : pays

note right of U
  **U = CATEGORY (union type)**
  Payer is a subset of Guardian U Sponsor, and each Payer row is exactly one of them.
  Discriminator: Payers.PayerType IN ('Guardian','Sponsor')
  Enforced by CK_Payers_ExactlyOne:
    Guardian -> GuardianId NOT NULL, SponsorId NULL
    Sponsor  -> SponsorId NOT NULL, GuardianId NULL
  One payer row per guardian / sponsor:
    IX_Payers_GuardianId, IX_Payers_SponsorId (filtered UNIQUE)
  Payer has its own surrogate key because the two superclasses
  have different keys (people.Persons vs finance.Sponsors).
end note

legend bottom
  single line = partial participation, thick line = total participation
  U = category (union), subset = subclass of superclass
endlegend
@enduml
